# Reconstructing the Internet Research Agency’s 2016 Social-Media Influence Operation

## Executive Summary

The supplied evidence supports a coherent reconstruction of a Russian influence operation in which the Internet Research Agency, identified here as **ENT-001**, operated from St. Petersburg, received funding and oversight through entities associated with **Yevgeniy Viktorovich Prigozhin (ENT-003)**, and functioned within the broader **Project Lakhta (ENT-002)** structure.

The operation combined:

- covert funding and organizational control;
- fake U.S. personas and stolen identities;
- U.S.-based servers, virtual private networks, and false email accounts;
- platform-specific activity across Facebook, Twitter, Instagram, and YouTube;
- organic posting, paid advertising, audience cultivation, and cross-platform amplification;
- targeting of racial, religious, ideological, regional, and political communities;
- efforts directed negatively at **Hillary Clinton (ENT-011)** and positively toward **Donald Trump (ENT-012)**, especially before the election;
- voter-suppression narratives, third-party promotion, political distrust, and offline rally mobilization.

The strongest demographic target documented in the Senate materials was African-Americans. The strongest candidate-level target was Hillary Clinton. Donald Trump was the principal documented candidate-level beneficiary of pre-election activity, although the broader operation was not limited to candidate advocacy. Much of the activity sought to increase distrust, disunity, anger, hopelessness, and social conflict.

The reconstruction must remain qualified. The DOJ indictment is a charging document describing alleged conduct, fake personas, concealed funding, infrastructure concealment, advertisements, identity theft, and rallies. The Senate reporting focuses more heavily on content, audience targeting, platform data, and political effects. The Senate also states that the available datasets were incomplete, that other Russian actors operated independently, and that the evidence does not establish that every divisive online post was created by the IRA.

## Scope and Evidence Standard

The reconstruction uses only the four supplied sources:

1. The [Internet Research Agency Indictment](https://www.justice.gov/d9/fieldable-panel-panes/basic-panes/attachments/2018/02/16/internet_research_agency_indictment.pdf) [1];
2. NPR’s report on the Senate findings, [Senate Report Finds Russians Used Social Media To Target Race In 2016](https://www.npr.org/2019/10/08/768319934/senate-report-russians-used-used-social-media-mostly-to-target-race-in-2016) [2];
3. the [U.S. Senate Select Committee on Intelligence report, Volume II](https://www.intelligence.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/sites-default-files-documents-report-volume2.pdf) [3]; and
4. the [Senate-document repository copy](https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/senatedocs/1/) [4].

The canonical entity and source identifiers are used as labels only when supported by the source text. Additional names appearing in the sources, such as MediaSintez, GlavSet, MixInfo, Azimut, NovInfo, Blacktivist, and Tennessee GOP, are described without assigning them new canonical entity IDs.

SRC-004 is a repository copy of the Senate material and should be treated as a corroborating access point rather than as a wholly independent investigation. NPR provides a secondary account of Senate findings. The DOJ indictment and Senate report therefore serve different evidentiary functions and should not be collapsed into a single type of proof.

## 1. Organizational Identity, Location, and Control

### Internet Research Agency and St. Petersburg base

The DOJ indictment describes ENT-001 as a Russian organization created to interfere with elections and political processes in the United States. It states that the organization registered with the Russian government around July 2013, began operating through Russian entities around June 2014, and occupied an office at 55 Savushkina Street in **St. Petersburg, Russia (ENT-006)**.

The indictment describes the St. Petersburg location as an operational hub with hundreds of employees and multiple departments, including graphics, data analysis, search-engine optimization, information technology, and finance. The organization had an annual budget equivalent to millions of U.S. dollars and described its purpose as conducting information warfare against the United States [1].

The Senate report likewise places ENT-001 in St. Petersburg and describes it as part of Russian active-measures campaigns operating since at least 2013. The Senate materials characterize the 2016 activity as a coordinated, multi-million-dollar campaign intended not only to influence the election but also to harm the United States and deepen social fractures [3][4].

### Project Lakhta

The evidence supports treating **Project Lakhta (ENT-002)** as the broader CONCORD-funded operation within which ENT-001 operated. The DOJ indictment states that ENT-004 funded ENT-001 as part of Project Lakhta, which contained both domestic Russian components and foreign-targeting components, including activity aimed at the United States [1].

By approximately September 2016, the monthly Project Lakhta budget submitted to CONCORD exceeded 73 million Russian rubles, or more than $1.25 million, including approximately one million rubles in bonuses [1].

The relationship is therefore best expressed as:

> **ENT-001 was part of ENT-002**, rather than treating ENT-001 and ENT-002 as interchangeable names in every context.

### Prigozhin and CONCORD

The DOJ indictment identifies **Yevgeniy Viktorovich Prigozhin (ENT-003)** as a Russian national who controlled **Concord Management and Consulting LLC (ENT-004)** and the broader CONCORD structure. It states that ENT-003 approved and supported ENT-001’s operations.

The indictment identifies ENT-004 and **Concord Catering (ENT-005)** as related Russian entities collectively referred to as CONCORD. CONCORD was described as ENT-001’s primary source of funding and as an organization that:

- controlled funding;
- recommended personnel;
- monitored reports;
- interacted with ENT-001 management; and
- oversaw operational activity.

The Senate report similarly states that ENT-001 was funded and directed by ENT-003 and links his financial role to broader Russian government support and authorization. Those findings concern the larger political and intelligence context; the direct operational funding chain is most specifically detailed in the DOJ indictment [1][3].

### Funding concealment

The DOJ indictment states that CONCORD concealed its involvement by labeling payments to ENT-001 as software support and development expenses. It also describes money being distributed through approximately fourteen bank accounts held in the names of CONCORD affiliates.

This concealment mechanism matters because the operation was not simply an open political campaign funded under its own name. The evidence supports a deliberate effort to obscure:

- who was paying for the activity;
- the Russian origin of the operation;
- the relationship between ENT-001 and CONCORD; and
- the connection between online activity, advertising, and offline events.

The supported control and funding graph is:

| Relationship | Supported linkage |
|---|---|
| `part_of_operation` | ENT-001 → ENT-002 |
| `funded_or_controlled` | ENT-003 → ENT-004 |
| `supported_or_promoted` | ENT-003 → ENT-001 |
| `funding_or_support_company` | ENT-004 → ENT-001 |
| `funding_or_support_company` | ENT-005 → ENT-001 as part of CONCORD |
| `based_in` | ENT-001 → ENT-006 |
| `used_platform` | ENT-001 → ENT-007, ENT-008, ENT-009, ENT-010 |

## 2. Operational Mechanics

### Fake personas and concealed identities

The DOJ indictment describes a sustained effort to make Russian-controlled accounts appear to be operated by real Americans. The operation included:

- false U.S. personas;
- fake social-media pages and groups;
- false claims that accounts were controlled by U.S. activists;
- stolen identities of real U.S. persons;
- U.S.-based email accounts registered under false names;
- stolen Social Security numbers, addresses, and birth dates;
- false identification documents;
- bank and PayPal accounts opened through stolen identities; and
- payments to real U.S. persons for promotional posts and rally-related work.

Some PayPal accounts were used to purchase Facebook advertisements. The indictment also states that real U.S. persons were sometimes paid between $25 and $50 for promotional posts on popular false-persona accounts [1].

The use of stolen identities and apparently authentic accounts served two purposes. First, it concealed Russian origin. Second, it allowed the accounts to develop credibility and audiences before delivering political messages.

### U.S.-based infrastructure

The indictment describes U.S.-based servers and virtual private networks used to mask Russian connections. Operatives connected from Russia through U.S.-based infrastructure to make activity appear domestic and to reduce the likelihood of detection.

The operation also used hundreds of U.S.-hosted email accounts linked to social-media profiles. This infrastructure supported account creation, advertising, communication with real U.S. persons, and the management of pages and groups.

This supports a clear operational inference: the infrastructure was not merely technical support for posting content. It was part of the deception architecture that made the accounts appear to be located in, operated from, and socially embedded within the United States.

### The translator project

The DOJ indictment describes a U.S.-focused department sometimes called the “translator project.” It began around April 2014 and grew to more than eighty employees by approximately July 2016.

The translator project operated on:

- Facebook;
- Twitter;
- Instagram; and
- YouTube.

Employees were instructed to:

- write according to U.S. time zones;
- use U.S. holidays and cultural references;
- imitate American political and social language;
- build accounts that appeared to be run by U.S. persons;
- track audience reactions;
- measure likes, reposts, comments, and audience growth; and
- support or exploit oppositional social movements.

The indictment states that the operation’s strategy included interfering with the 2016 presidential election and spreading distrust toward candidates and the political system generally [1].

### Intelligence collection and audience research

The indictment describes two employees traveling to the United States in June 2014 under false pretenses. They visited multiple states, purchased cameras and communication devices, discussed evacuation scenarios, and produced an intelligence report after the trip.

The operation also gathered information online. In one example, an operative posing as a U.S. person learned from a Texas grassroots organization that the campaign should focus on “purple states” such as Colorado, Virginia, and Florida [1].

The Senate report confirms that ENT-001 studied U.S. social-media groups, tracked audience behavior, and measured engagement. This indicates that content selection was not random. It was informed by audience research, geographic targeting, and the identification of communities likely to respond to emotionally charged subjects.

## 3. Platform-by-Platform Reconstruction

## Facebook

Facebook served as a central platform for audience development, paid advertising, group and page management, event promotion, and offline mobilization.

The Senate dataset included approximately:

- 61,500 organic Facebook posts;
- 3,393 paid advertisements;
- 81 ENT-001-attributed pages;
- approximately 3.3 million followers of ENT-001-backed pages; and
- approximately 76.5 million engagements.

Facebook estimated that as many as 126 million Americans encountered ENT-001-manufactured content between 2015 and 2017. That is an exposure estimate, not a finding that every person understood, believed, or acted on the material [3].

Facebook also identified at least 130 real-world events promoted through ENT-001 activity. The Senate report states that:

- approximately 338,300 genuine Facebook accounts engaged with event-promoting content;
- approximately 62,500 users indicated an intention to attend; and
- approximately 25,800 users expressed interest.

A particularly significant example occurred in Houston in May 2016. The “Heart of Texas” page promoted an anti-Islam protest, while “United Muslims for America” promoted a simultaneous counter-event at the same Islamic Da’wah Center. The evidence supports an inference that the operation sometimes used opposing pages to encourage confrontation between real-world groups [3].

Facebook advertising was also used for direct political messaging. The DOJ indictment describes advertisements supporting Trump and attacking Clinton, including messages such as:

- `#HillaryClintonIsNotMyPresident`;
- `#HillaryClintonForPrison2016`;
- `#Trump2016`; and
- messages portraying Trump as the only candidate who could defend police from terrorists.

For the “Florida Goes Trump” campaign, the indictment states that advertisements reached more than 59,000 Facebook users in Florida and generated more than 8,300 clicks to the “Being Patriotic” page [1].

The Senate report states that Facebook estimated 11.4 million people in the United States saw at least one of the 3,393 advertisements attributed to ENT-001. Approximately 44 percent of impressions occurred before November 8, 2016, and 56 percent occurred after the election. Approximately one-quarter of the ads were never seen. About one-quarter were targeted at the state, city, or university level [3].

The evidence therefore supports two conclusions:

1. Facebook advertising was operationally significant and documented; but
2. paid advertising alone understates the operation because organic content and page-based audience cultivation were more extensive.

## Instagram

Instagram appears to have been the operation’s strongest platform by audience engagement and per-post performance.

The Senate report attributes to ENT-001:

- 133 Instagram accounts;
- more than 116,000 posts;
- approximately 3.3 million followers; and
- approximately 187 million engagements.

Twelve accounts exceeded 100,000 followers, and nearly half of the 133 accounts exceeded 10,000 followers. The report states that Instagram outperformed Facebook in reach, likes, and per-post engagement according to the measures reviewed by the Committee [3].

Examples of large accounts included:

- `@Blackstagram_`, with more than 300,000 followers and more than 28 million interactions;
- `@american.veterans`, with 215,680 followers and nearly 18.5 million engagements;
- `@sincerely_black_`, with approximately 196,754 followers;
- `@rainbow_nation_us`, with approximately 156,465 followers;
- `@afrokingdom_`, with approximately 150,511 followers;
- `@pray4police`, with approximately 127,853 followers; and
- `@feminism_tag`, with approximately 126,605 followers.

The account portfolio shows how ENT-001 created multiple audience identities rather than relying on one central political brand. The content could be presented as Black activism, veterans’ advocacy, police support, feminism, LGBT rights, regional identity, or other issue-based activity.

## Twitter

Twitter was the earliest documented platform for ENT-001’s U.S.-focused influence activity. The Senate report states that ENT-001 or a predecessor posted on Twitter as early as 2009, primarily in Russian for a domestic audience. Accounts targeting U.S. audiences with English-language content emerged in 2013 [3].

Twitter was used for:

- high-volume posting;
- real-time political commentary;
- hashtag games;
- amplification;
- persona development;
- news aggregation;
- cross-platform promotion; and
- attacks on both sides of divisive issues.

Twitter identified more than 3,800 accounts tied to ENT-001. The Senate report describes nearly 8.5 million tweets and approximately 72 million engagements, with about 1.4 million users engaging with ENT-001-originated tweets. Approximately 57 percent of the posts were in Russian and 36 percent were in English. Elsewhere, the report refers to a broader dataset of approximately 10.4 million tweets, suggesting a difference in dataset scope or methodology [3].

The Senate report identifies several account categories:

- **Right Troll** accounts that strongly supported Donald Trump and attacked Democrats;
- **Left Troll** accounts focused on cultural identity and African-American audiences and attacking Hillary Clinton;
- **News Feed** accounts presented as local news aggregators;
- **Hashtag Gamer** accounts that played hashtag games to provoke reactions; and
- **Fearmonger** accounts that spread sensational or fabricated narratives.

Important false personas included:

- `@TEN_GOP`, which falsely presented itself as the Tennessee Republican Party;
- `@Jenn_Abrams`, a prominent conservative-appearing persona; and
- `@wokeluisa`, a left-leaning persona.

The Senate report states that these accounts sometimes attacked and defended both sides of the same issue, including NFL protests, and could be linked to activity from the same IP address. This supports the conclusion that the operation was designed not simply to advocate one consistent policy position but to intensify conflict and provoke reactions.

## YouTube

ENT-001 began posting videos on YouTube as early as September 2015. The Senate report identifies:

- more than 1,100 videos;
- approximately 43 hours of content;
- seventeen channels; and
- ten channels containing 1,063 videos, approximately 96 percent of which focused on race and police brutality.

Channel names included:

- Black Matters;
- BlackToLive;
- Cop Block US;
- Don’t Shoot; and
- PoliceState.

Only 25 videos had election-related keywords in their titles. All politically oriented videos identified in the report were opposed to Hillary Clinton. Some discouraged African-American participation in the election, while others encouraged African-Americans to vote for Jill Stein [3].

YouTube therefore appears to have functioned less as a broad candidate-advertising platform and more as a vehicle for racial, police, and social-conflict narratives that could later support voter-suppression or anti-Clinton messaging.

## Cross-Platform Design

The platforms were not isolated channels. The evidence supports a coordinated division of labor:

- **Facebook** developed pages, groups, events, advertisements, and deeper audience relationships.
- **Instagram** distributed highly engaging visual content and reached large identity-based communities.
- **Twitter** generated volume, real-time reactions, hashtags, amplification, and persona visibility.
- **YouTube** distributed longer-form video content focused heavily on race, police brutality, and Black Lives Matter.

The accounts were also cross-promoted. The DOJ indictment describes auxiliary accounts used to promote main accounts through links and reposts. The Senate report describes a progression from character development and confidence building to audience cultivation and later delivery of “payload” content.

## 4. Audience Targeting and Content Strategy

### African-American audiences

The strongest demographic emphasis in the Senate findings was African-Americans. NPR summarized the Committee’s conclusion that there was an “overwhelming operational emphasis on race” and that no group was targeted more than African-Americans [2].

The Senate report states that:

- more than 66 percent of ENT-001’s Facebook advertisements contained a race-related term;
- geographic targeting was principally directed at African-Americans in key metropolitan areas;
- the Blacktivist Facebook page generated approximately 11.2 million engagements;
- five of the ten largest Instagram accounts focused on African-American issues or audiences; and
- approximately 96 percent of ENT-001’s YouTube content concerned race and police brutality [3].

The campaign sometimes moved beyond content distribution. The Senate report describes attempts to persuade targets to:

- sign petitions;
- provide personal information;
- participate in self-defense training; and
- engage with businesses or activists through identity-based pages.

For example, the “Black4Black” operation solicited personal information from African-American-owned businesses in exchange for free social-media promotion. The “BlackFist” page paid an African-American activist approximately $700 to teach twelve self-defense classes [3].

These examples show that the campaign sought not only to influence opinions but also to create relationships, gather information, and stimulate behavior.

### Other issue communities

The operation also developed pages and groups centered on:

- immigration;
- religion;
- veterans;
- police;
- sexual identity;
- regional identity;
- Black Lives Matter;
- Muslim identity;
- LGBT issues; and
- support for or opposition to political movements.

Named examples in the DOJ indictment include “Secured Borders,” “Blacktivist,” “United Muslims of America,” “Army of Jesus,” “South United,” and “Heart of Texas” [1].

This portfolio allowed ENT-001 to present different political identities to different audiences. The same overarching operation could appear conservative, progressive, religious, anti-police, pro-police, pro-Trump, anti-Trump, or focused on a specific racial or regional community.

### Candidate-level objectives

The sources support a clear pre-election pattern:

- Hillary Clinton was targeted negatively across left-leaning and right-leaning personas.
- Donald Trump received mostly positive attention before the election.
- Other candidates, including Ted Cruz and Marco Rubio, were also attacked or disparaged.
- Bernie Sanders received some supportive treatment.
- Some content encouraged people not to vote, to vote for Jill Stein, or to become cynical about the electoral process.

The DOJ indictment states that ENT-001’s operations primarily communicated derogatory information about Hillary Clinton and supported Trump. The Senate report states that Clinton was the only presidential candidate whose references were uniformly negative [1][3].

The evidence therefore supports the following candidate-level relationship:

> **ENT-001 targeted against ENT-011 and supported or promoted ENT-012, especially before the election.**

That relationship should not be overstated into a claim that the campaign single-handedly determined the election result. The supplied sources document activity, messaging, audience engagement, and mobilization. They do not establish a complete causal estimate of electoral impact.

### Voter suppression and third-party promotion

The DOJ indictment and Senate report identify three broad categories of voter-suppression messaging:

1. discouraging turnout or encouraging election boycotts;
2. encouraging support for a third-party candidate, particularly Jill Stein; and
3. attacking Clinton in ways aimed at nonwhite audiences or likely Clinton voters.

Examples include messages from “Woke Blacks,” “Blacktivist,” and “United Muslims of America” encouraging people not to vote for Clinton, not to vote at all, or to vote for Jill Stein [1][3].

These messages fit within the broader objective of reducing trust in the political system. The operation did not need every audience member to support Trump directly. It could also advance its objectives by discouraging participation, increasing cynicism, or intensifying hostility toward Clinton and the electoral process.

### Content before direct political messaging

The Senate report states that most ENT-001 content did not explicitly support one presidential candidate. Candidate references appeared in approximately:

- 6 percent of analyzed tweets;
- 18 percent of Instagram posts; and
- 7 percent of Facebook posts.

The report cautions that low candidate-mention rates do not disprove political intent. Innocuous or nonpolitical material could be used to establish credible personas, cultivate audiences, and build trust before tailored political content was deployed [3].

This is a central feature of the operation: the political payload often came after the persona had already become credible.

## 5. Offline Mobilization and Real-World Effects

The evidence documents multiple instances in which online personas interacted with real U.S. persons and helped organize or promote offline activity.

The DOJ indictment and Senate report describe:

- “March for Trump” in New York on June 25, 2016;
- “Down with Hillary” in New York on July 23, 2016;
- “Florida Goes Trump” rallies on August 20, 2016;
- “Support Hillary. Save American Muslims” in Washington, D.C., on July 9, 2016;
- post-election rallies supporting President-elect Trump;
- “Trump is NOT my President” protests; and
- “Charlotte Against Trump.”

ENT-001 used Facebook pages, Twitter accounts, Instagram accounts, advertisements, direct messages, press releases, and false personas to promote these events.

The operation also paid real U.S. persons to perform tasks such as:

- building a cage on a flatbed truck; and
- wearing a costume portraying Hillary Clinton in a prison uniform.

For the “Florida Goes Trump” campaign, the indictment states that Facebook advertising reached more than 59,000 users in Florida and generated more than 8,300 clicks to the “Being Patriotic” page [1].

The Senate report describes the campaign as provoking real-world events. The documented evidence supports claims of mobilization, event promotion, and audience engagement. It does not, by itself, establish that every event was attended by large numbers of people or that the operation controlled the resulting behavior of participants.

## 6. Chronology

### 2013–2014: Formation and intelligence gathering

The evidence places the organization’s formation and Russian operations around 2013–2014. U.S.-focused Twitter activity began by 2013, and the U.S.-focused translator project developed around April 2014.

In June 2014, employees traveled to the United States under false pretenses, gathering information about political geography, communications, and potential target states.

### 2015–mid-2016: Persona and audience development

By 2015, ENT-001 was using Facebook and Instagram pages, Twitter accounts, YouTube channels, advertisements, and issue-based groups to establish apparently authentic audiences.

The campaign developed content around race, police, religion, immigration, veterans, regional identity, LGBT issues, and other divisive subjects. Employees tracked engagement and adjusted content to audience reactions.

### Mid- to late 2016: Political and offline mobilization

By 2016, the operation had added more direct political content, including anti-Clinton and pro-Trump advertisements, voter-suppression narratives, rallies, direct contacts with real U.S. persons, and attempts to interact with Trump campaign supporters and grassroots organizations.

The strongest documented candidate-level pattern was anti-Clinton activity combined with pre-election support for Trump.

### After Election Day 2016

The campaign did not stop after the election. The Senate materials state that:

- Instagram activity increased by approximately 238 percent;
- Facebook activity increased by approximately 59 percent;
- Twitter activity increased by approximately 52 percent; and
- YouTube citations increased by approximately 84 percent.

Post-election content included:

- support for President-elect Trump;
- anti-Trump activity from left-leaning personas;
- `#Impeach45`;
- `#Resist`;
- `#GunReformNow`;
- NFL-kneeling controversy content;
- anti-FBI hashtags such as `#ReleaseTheMemo`;
- protests against Trump; and
- continued exploitation of racial, ideological, and social divisions [3][4].

The apparent shift from pre-election pro-Trump content to post-election anti-Trump content is best understood as a change in tactical emphasis. The operation could support Trump before the election while later using anti-Trump content to provoke additional conflict and polarization.

## 7. Documented Effects and Limits of Attribution

The supplied sources document several types of operational effect:

- large-scale exposure and engagement;
- growth of pages, groups, and accounts;
- audience interaction with posts, advertisements, and videos;
- event promotion and expressions of intent to attend;
- recruitment and payment of real U.S. persons;
- petitions, information collection, and self-defense training;
- voter-suppression narratives;
- third-party promotion;
- cross-platform amplification; and
- increased activity after Election Day.

The evidence does not support several stronger claims:

- that ENT-001 created every divisive online post;
- that all Russian influence activity came from ENT-001;
- that every account identified by a platform was definitively controlled by ENT-001;
- that the platform datasets captured the full operation;
- that engagement necessarily equaled persuasion;
- that exposure necessarily produced behavioral change; or
- that the operation alone caused the 2016 election result.

The Senate Committee expressly states that the full scope of Russian activity remained unknown. It relied substantially on platform companies to identify accounts and provide data, did not independently determine the accuracy of every platform investigation, and recognized that the available data likely did not include all ENT-001 activity [3].

The Senate report also identifies other Russian vectors, including Russian government-funded or government-directed actors outside ENT-001. Those actors are outside the canonical entity set used here and are not merged into the ENT-001 relationship graph.

## 8. Required Proof Steps

### Step 1: Identify the primary target and gather independent public sources

The strongest candidate-level target supported by the sources is **ENT-011, Hillary Clinton**. The strongest candidate-level beneficiary was **ENT-012, Donald Trump**, particularly before the election. The strongest demographic emphasis was African-American audiences.

The DOJ indictment supplies detailed operational facts. The Senate report supplies platform data, audience analysis, and political-effect findings. NPR summarizes the Senate conclusions, while the repository copy provides an accessible copy of the Senate material.

### Step 2: Link aliases, organizations, people, platforms, and infrastructure

The supported chain is:

- ENT-001 operated from ENT-006;
- ENT-001 was funded as part of ENT-002;
- ENT-003 controlled ENT-004 and supported ENT-001;
- ENT-004 and ENT-005 collectively formed the CONCORD funding and oversight structure;
- ENT-001 used ENT-007, ENT-008, ENT-009, and ENT-010;
- ENT-001 used fake personas, stolen identities, U.S.-based infrastructure, and concealed payment channels; and
- ENT-001 targeted ENT-011 while supporting or promoting ENT-012, especially before the election.

### Step 3: Produce traceable findings with preserved caveats

The DOJ indictment and Senate findings should be used together but not treated as interchangeable. The indictment emphasizes organizational structure, funding, deception, infrastructure, advertisements, and rallies. The Senate findings emphasize content, audience targeting, platform data, demographic effects, political objectives, and the limitations of available evidence.

The central caveat is that the supplied evidence establishes a documented IRA operation, not universal authorship of all divisive online content.

## 9. Contradictions and Unresolved Measurement Differences

The controlling source-level distinction is that the DOJ indictment emphasizes fake personas and concealed funding, while the Senate reporting emphasizes content, audience targeting, and political effects. This is a difference in evidentiary focus rather than a reason to discard either source.

The evidence also contains several unresolved measurement differences:

- The DOJ indictment documents significant paid advertising, while the Senate report concludes that organic content was operationally more important.
- Instagram engagement totals, follower counts, and estimated reach are not fully reconciled and may measure different concepts.
- The Senate report refers both to nearly 8.5 million tweets from identified accounts and to a broader dataset of approximately 10.4 million tweets.
- The Senate materials contain a discrepancy concerning whether ENT-001 used Facebook Custom Audiences.
- Pre-election pro-Trump activity and post-election anti-Trump activity appear different, but the difference is consistent with a strategy of using opposing personas to intensify conflict over time.

These issues should be preserved as limitations or scope distinctions rather than converted into unsupported conclusions.

